  "notes": "West leads the ♣K against 4♥. The defense takes 2 club tricks and the third round is ruffed by East. South wins the spade shift and draws trump.\n\nSouth now has a complete count on the hand. East started with 6 spades (for his weak 2 bid), 2 hearts, and 2 clubs. East must have exactly 3 diamonds. The ♦Q will not drop under the ♦A and ♦K, meaning South has a sure diamond loser.\n\nIf East started with the ♦Q he can be trump squeezed. South leads out all but 1 of his hearts. On South's second last heart, a diamond is thrown from dummy and East is squeezed.\n\nA diamond discard will make the ♦Q drop under the ♦A and ♦K. South has a trump remaining to cross to his hand and enjoy the ♦J. A spade discard will allow South to cross to the dummy and establish a spade trick by ruffing. Dummy has a diamond entry to cash the good spade.\n\nThe simple trump squeeze matures with 1 loser. A defender is squeezed in 2 suits, 1 of which can be established by ruffing if the defender pitches from that suit. The other suit will become blocked at the time of the squeeze. The hand opposite the trump must have at least 2 entries. This type of squeeze is automatic, succeeding against either defender.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♣K against 4♥. The defense takes 2 club tricks and the third round is ruffed by East. South wins the spade shift and draws trump.",
